# Custom Flows

## Overview

Your refund desk needs rules that the model can't talk its way around: no
refund without an order, no refund on an order above the desk limit, and no
refund larger than the order total. This Livebook builds that desk as your own
root reasoning Flow, `MyApp.RefundFlow`, from the public Jido AI Actions plus
three Actions of your own. By the end, this works:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
signal =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "The blender in order A-1001 arrived broken. Please refund it.",
    order_id: "A-1001",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output["amount_cents"]}
# => {:completed, 4500}
```

A built-in Flow such as `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct` fixes its steps. A custom
Flow lets you put deterministic checks before and after the model call, give
the model extra facts, and decide which outcomes reach the Session.
[Choose a Reasoning Flow](choose-a-reasoning-flow.md) covers when a built-in
Flow is enough.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

`plug` provides `Req.Test`, which the offline model uses.

## The Root Flow Contract

The Agent's `jido.ai.request` route runs the root Flow directly. Any Flow
module can be the root if it meets this contract:

| Part | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Input schema | A Zoi object with an `input` field. It owns every request field, its default, and its validation. |
| `reasoning` defaults | Each name in the Agent's `reasoning MyFlow do ... end` block must be a field of the input schema. `input` can't be a default. |
| Output | `%{state: map(), response: map()}`. `state` becomes the next Agent state; `response` becomes the `jido.ai.response` reply data. |
| Session | To keep the conversation, read `state.session` and return the updated Session in `state.session`. |

`Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.StartRequest` and
`Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.FinalizeTurn` handle the last two rows for you.

## The Building Blocks

Every built-in Flow is assembled from these public Actions. Read
`Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfThought` and `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ChainOfThought.ModelRound`
for the smallest complete example.

| Action | Takes | Returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `StartRequest` | Request fields and budgets | The turn: a map that carries the Session, model, options, budgets, and counters through the Flow |
| `GuardInput` | `turn` | `%{allowed: boolean, reason_code: atom, reason: string \| nil}` |
| `DecideInput` | `turn`, `guard` | `%{kind: :model, input: %{turn: turn}}` or `%{kind: :complete, output: %{turn: turn, result: result}}` |
| `ProjectConversation` | `turn` | `%{turn: turn, context: %Jido.AI.Context{}}` with instructions and projected Thread messages |
| `CallModel` | `turn`, `context`, optional `tools: false` | `%{turn: turn, outcome: outcome, context: context}` |
| `GuardOutput` | `turn`, `outcome` | `%{allowed: boolean, reason: string}` |
| `RecordModelAttempt` | `turn`, `outcome`, `context`, `output_allowed`, `output_reason` | `%{turn: turn}`; appends the assistant message only when `output_allowed` is `true` |
| `FinalizeTurn` | `outcome: %{turn: turn, result: result}` | `%{state: state, response: response}` |

The `result` in each terminal outcome comes from `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Result`:
`completed/2`, `blocked/3`, `failed/3`, or `new/5`. `FinalizeTurn` copies its
`status`, `output`, `reason_code`, `reason`, and `reasoning` into the reply.

`StartRequest` accepts these params:

| Param | Required | Default when absent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `input`, `instructions`, `model`, `options`, `context_window`, `max_model_calls` | Yes | — |
| `flow` | No | `nil`; reported as `reply.data.flow` |
| `fallback_models` | No | `[]` |
| `output_schema` | No | `nil`; a JSON Schema map turns on strict structured output |
| `max_retries` | No | `0` |
| `max_elapsed_ms`, `max_input_tokens`, `max_output_tokens`, `max_total_tokens`, `max_total_cost` | No | `nil` (no limit) |
| `prompt_cache` | No | `%{enabled: false}` |
| `stream` | No | `false` |
| `tool_policy`, `review_tools`, `max_tool_rounds`, `max_tool_calls`, `max_tool_output_bytes` | No | Tool settings; this desk has no tools |

`context_window` is `%{max_messages: integer, max_bytes: integer}`.
`CallModel` checks `max_model_calls` and the time, token, and cost budgets
before it sends a request, and returns a `status: :budget` outcome instead of
calling the model when one is spent.

## Offline Model

This is the fake provider from [Getting Started](getting-started.livemd),
with one addition: each request body is sent to this notebook so you can read
what the model received.

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.OfflineModel do
  @moduledoc "Queues fake OpenAI Responses API replies for ReqLLM."

  @doc "Request options that send model calls to this module."
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 256,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
    }
  end

  @doc "Queues one reply per model call and reports each request body."
  def respond_with(bodies) do
    owner = self()

    Enum.each(bodies, fn body ->
      Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
        {:ok, request, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
        send(owner, {:model_request, Jason.decode!(request)})
        Req.Test.json(conn, body)
      end)
    end)
  end

  @doc "A final text answer. Structured output arrives as JSON text."
  def text(text) do
    %{
      "id" => "resp_1",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "error" => nil,
      "incomplete_details" => nil,
      "parallel_tool_calls" => true,
      "output" => [
        %{
          "type" => "message",
          "id" => "msg_1",
          "role" => "assistant",
          "status" => "completed",
          "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
        }
      ],
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 20,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 5,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 25
      }
    }
  end

  @doc "Returns the request bodies received so far."
  def requests(acc \\ []) do
    receive do
      {:model_request, body} -> requests([body | acc])
    after
      0 -> Enum.reverse(acc)
    end
  end
end
```

## Orders and Output

The desk reads orders from your system. Here it is a fixed map:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Orders do
  @orders %{
    "A-1001" => %{order_id: "A-1001", status: "delivered", total_cents: 4_500},
    "A-2002" => %{order_id: "A-2002", status: "delivered", total_cents: 89_000}
  }

  def fetch(order_id), do: Map.fetch(@orders, order_id)
end
```

The model answers with one refund decision. Define it once in Zoi and send
the JSON Schema form to the model:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.RefundDecision do
  @schema Zoi.object(
            %{
              decision: Zoi.enum(refund: "refund", deny: "deny"),
              amount_cents: Zoi.integer(gte: 0),
              message: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.min(1)
            },
            coerce: true,
            unrecognized_keys: :error
          )

  def schema, do: @schema
  def json_schema, do: Zoi.to_json_schema(@schema)
end
```

`StartRequest` takes `output_schema` as a JSON Schema map. A Zoi struct there
isn't a JSON Schema.

## Pre-Model Policy

The first guardrail runs before any model work. It reads the `GuardInput`
result, then checks the order:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Refund.CheckPolicy do
  @moduledoc "Blocks refund requests that break desk policy before the model runs."

  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/refund/check_policy",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "refund_check_policy",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        guard: Zoi.map(),
        order_id: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.nullable(),
        max_cents: Zoi.integer(gte: 0)
      }),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{guard: %{allowed: false} = guard}, _context),
    do: {:ok, Map.put(guard, :order, nil)}

  def run(%{order_id: nil}, _context),
    do: {:ok, blocked("a refund request needs an order_id")}

  def run(%{order_id: order_id, max_cents: max_cents}, _context) do
    case MyApp.Orders.fetch(order_id) do
      :error ->
        {:ok, blocked("order #{order_id} was not found")}

      {:ok, %{total_cents: total}} when total > max_cents ->
        {:ok, blocked("order total #{total} cents is above the desk limit of #{max_cents} cents")}

      {:ok, order} ->
        {:ok, %{allowed: true, reason_code: :none, reason: nil, order: order}}
    end
  end

  defp blocked(reason), do: %{allowed: false, reason_code: :policy, reason: reason, order: nil}
end
```

It returns the `GuardInput` shape plus the order. `DecideInput` would pass
only the turn to the model round, and the round needs the order, so the desk
has its own decision and expander for an `invoke`:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Refund.Route do
  @moduledoc "Starts the model round or ends the turn with the policy result."

  alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.Result

  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/refund/route",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "refund_route",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{turn: Zoi.any(), policy: Zoi.map()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{turn: turn, policy: %{allowed: true, order: order}}, _context),
    do: {:ok, %{kind: :model, input: %{turn: turn, order: order}}}

  def run(%{turn: turn, policy: policy}, _context) do
    result = Result.blocked(policy.reason_code, policy.reason)
    {:ok, %{kind: :complete, output: %{turn: turn, result: result}}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.Refund.Expand do
  @moduledoc "Expands the route decision into its next executable."

  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/refund/expand",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "refund_expand",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{kind: :complete, output: output}, _context), do: {:ok, output}
  def run(%{kind: :model, input: input}, _context), do: {:continue, input, MyApp.RefundRound}
end
```

An `invoke` runs the decision, then lets the expander return a final value
or `{:continue, input, target}`. Only the selected target runs, so a blocked
request never reaches the model. A `choice` can't do this: its options must
be Action modules, and the model round is a Flow.

## Post-Model Checks

Three Actions run inside the model round. The first gives the model the order
facts through a stable `Jido.AI.Context.transform/3` operation:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Refund.AddOrderFacts do
  @moduledoc "Adds the order facts to the open model context."

  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/refund/add_order_facts",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "refund_add_order_facts",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{context: Zoi.any(), order: Zoi.map()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{context: context, order: order}, _context) do
    facts =
      "Order #{order.order_id} is #{order.status}. Its total is #{order.total_cents} cents."

    with {:ok, context} <-
           Jido.AI.Context.transform(context, "refund-order-facts", fn req_llm_context ->
             %{req_llm_context | messages: req_llm_context.messages ++ [ReqLLM.Context.system(facts)]}
           end) do
      {:ok, %{context: context}}
    end
  end
end
```

The second checks the structured output against the order. It runs after
`GuardOutput` and before `RecordModelAttempt`, so a rejected decision never
becomes an assistant message in the Session:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Refund.CheckAmount do
  @moduledoc "Rejects a refund larger than the order total."

  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/refund/check_amount",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "refund_check_amount",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{guard: Zoi.map(), outcome: Zoi.map(), order: Zoi.map()}),
    output_schema:
      Zoi.object(%{allowed: Zoi.boolean(), reason: Zoi.string(), over_total: Zoi.boolean()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{guard: %{allowed: false, reason: reason}}, _context),
    do: {:ok, %{allowed: false, reason: reason, over_total: false}}

  def run(%{outcome: %{output: %{"amount_cents" => amount}}, order: order}, _context)
      when amount > order.total_cents do
    reason = "refund of #{amount} cents exceeds the order total of #{order.total_cents} cents"
    {:ok, %{allowed: false, reason: reason, over_total: true}}
  end

  def run(_params, _context), do: {:ok, %{allowed: true, reason: "", over_total: false}}
end
```

The third turns the recorded attempt into the terminal outcome. `Escalate`
handles the over-total case; `Complete` handles everything else, the same way
the Chain of Thought round does:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Refund.Complete do
  @moduledoc "Builds the terminal result from one recorded model attempt."

  alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.Result

  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/refund/complete",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "refund_complete",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{turn: Zoi.any()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{turn: %{model_outcome: %{status: :error, reason: reason}} = turn}, _context),
    do: {:ok, %{turn: turn, result: Result.failed(:model, reason)}}

  def run(%{turn: %{model_outcome: %{status: :budget, reason: reason}} = turn}, _context),
    do: {:ok, %{turn: turn, result: Result.blocked(:limits, reason)}}

  def run(%{turn: %{output_allowed: false} = turn}, _context),
    do: {:ok, %{turn: turn, result: Result.blocked(:output, turn.output_reason)}}

  def run(%{turn: turn}, _context),
    do: {:ok, %{turn: turn, result: Result.completed(turn.model_outcome.output)}}
end

defmodule MyApp.Refund.Escalate do
  @moduledoc "Ends the turn when the model offers more than the order total."

  alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.Result

  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/refund/escalate",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "refund_escalate",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{turn: Zoi.any()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{turn: turn}, _context),
    do: {:ok, %{turn: turn, result: Result.blocked(:policy, turn.output_reason)}}
end
```

## The Model Round

The round is a Flow. It projects the Session, adds the order facts, calls the
model once without tools, runs both output checks, records the attempt, and
picks the terminal outcome with a `choice`:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.RefundRound do
  @moduledoc "Runs one guarded refund decision model call."

  alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.{CallModel, GuardOutput, ProjectConversation, RecordModelAttempt}
  alias MyApp.Refund.{AddOrderFacts, CheckAmount, Complete, Escalate}

  use Jido.Flow,
    id: "my_app/refund_round",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "my_app_refund_round",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{turn: Zoi.any(), order: Zoi.map()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "project",
      action: ProjectConversation,
      params: %{turn: input(:turn)}

    step "order_facts",
      action: AddOrderFacts,
      params: %{context: result("project", :context), order: input(:order)}

    step "model",
      action: CallModel,
      params: %{
        turn: result("project", :turn),
        context: result("order_facts", :context),
        tools: false
      }

    step "output_guard",
      action: GuardOutput,
      params: %{turn: result("model", :turn), outcome: result("model", :outcome)}

    step "amount_check",
      action: CheckAmount,
      params: %{
        guard: result("output_guard"),
        outcome: result("model", :outcome),
        order: input(:order)
      }

    step "record_attempt",
      action: RecordModelAttempt,
      params: %{
        turn: result("model", :turn),
        outcome: result("model", :outcome),
        context: result("model", :context),
        output_allowed: result("amount_check", :allowed),
        output_reason: result("amount_check", :reason)
      }

    choice "finish" do
      option "over_total",
        condition: result("amount_check", :over_total),
        action: Escalate,
        params: %{turn: result("record_attempt", :turn)}

      otherwise action: Complete, params: %{turn: result("record_attempt", :turn)}
    end

    output result("finish")
  end
end
```

A `choice` runs the first option whose condition is `true`, or `otherwise`.
The condition reads a Boolean that `CheckAmount` declares in its output
schema. A `nil` condition fails the Flow, so test a declared Boolean rather
than the model output itself. See the Jido Action
[Choices](https://hexdocs.pm/jido_action/flow-choices.html) guide.

## The Root Flow

The root Flow owns the request schema and wires the parts together:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.RefundFlow do
  @moduledoc "The refund desk root reasoning Flow."

  alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.{FinalizeTurn, GuardInput, StartRequest}
  alias MyApp.Refund.{CheckPolicy, Expand, Route}

  @instructions """
  You decide refund requests for the support desk. Refund at most the order
  total. Deny requests that give no reason. Return one decision.
  """

  use Jido.Flow,
    id: "my_app/refund_flow",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "my_app_refund_flow",
    description: "A policy-checked refund decision",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(
        %{
          input: Zoi.string(),
          order_id: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.nullable() |> Zoi.default(nil),
          instructions: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default(@instructions),
          model: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.default("openai:gpt-4o-mini"),
          options:
            Zoi.map()
            |> Zoi.default(%{max_tokens: 256, receive_timeout: 30_000, temperature: 0.0}),
          refund:
            Zoi.object(
              %{max_cents: Zoi.integer(gte: 0) |> Zoi.default(50_000)},
              unrecognized_keys: :error
            )
            |> Zoi.default(%{max_cents: 50_000}),
          context:
            Zoi.object(
              %{
                max_messages: Zoi.integer(gte: 1, lte: 512) |> Zoi.default(32),
                max_bytes: Zoi.integer(gte: 1_024, lte: 2_000_000) |> Zoi.default(100_000)
              },
              unrecognized_keys: :error
            )
            |> Zoi.default(%{max_messages: 32, max_bytes: 100_000}),
          limits:
            Zoi.object(
              %{
                elapsed_ms: Zoi.integer(gte: 1, lte: 600_000) |> Zoi.default(60_000),
                total_tokens: Zoi.integer(gte: 1) |> Zoi.nullable() |> Zoi.default(nil),
                total_cost: Zoi.number(gte: 0) |> Zoi.nullable() |> Zoi.default(nil)
              },
              unrecognized_keys: :error
            )
            |> Zoi.default(%{elapsed_ms: 60_000, total_tokens: nil, total_cost: nil})
        },
        unrecognized_keys: :error
      ),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{state: Zoi.map(), response: Zoi.map()})

  flow do
    step "decision_schema", [] do
      {:ok, %{json: MyApp.RefundDecision.json_schema()}}
    end

    step "start_request",
      action: StartRequest,
      params: %{
        flow: %{id: "my_app/refund_flow", vsn: 1},
        input: input(:input),
        instructions: input(:instructions),
        model: input(:model),
        options: input(:options),
        output_schema: result("decision_schema", :json),
        context_window: input(:context),
        max_model_calls: 1,
        max_elapsed_ms: input([:limits, :elapsed_ms]),
        max_total_tokens: input([:limits, :total_tokens]),
        max_total_cost: input([:limits, :total_cost])
      }

    step "input_guard",
      action: GuardInput,
      params: %{turn: result("start_request")}

    step "policy",
      action: CheckPolicy,
      params: %{
        guard: result("input_guard"),
        order_id: input(:order_id),
        max_cents: input([:refund, :max_cents])
      }

    invoke "refund",
      decision: Route,
      expander: Expand,
      params: %{turn: result("start_request"), policy: result("policy")}

    step "finalize",
      action: FinalizeTurn,
      params: %{outcome: result("refund")}

    output result("finalize")
  end
end
```

| Choice in the schema | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `order_id` is nullable with default `nil` | A missing order reaches `CheckPolicy` and gets a `:blocked` reply, not a schema error. |
| `refund.max_cents` | A desk setting you can default per Agent. |
| `limits` has no `model_calls` | The Flow fixes `max_model_calls: 1`; callers can't raise it. |
| `output_schema` isn't an input field | Callers can't change the decision shape. |
| `unrecognized_keys: :error` | A request with an unknown field fails validation. |

## Select the Flow

Reference the Flow from the Agent. The block sets defaults for fields of
`MyApp.RefundFlow`'s input schema, including your own `refund` object:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "support_agent",
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning MyApp.RefundFlow do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"

      refund do
        max_cents 20_000
      end

      limits do
        total_tokens 2_000
      end
    end
  end
end
```

A name the schema doesn't define fails the build. `refund do currency "usd" end`
raises `reasoning configuration is invalid for MyApp.RefundFlow at refund.currency`.

Inspect the result without calling a model:

```elixir
explained = MyApp.SupportAgent.explain()

{explained.reasoning, explained.defaults, explained.tools}
```

The result is
`{MyApp.RefundFlow, %{model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini", refund: %{max_cents: 20000}, limits: %{total_tokens: 2000}}, []}`.

```elixir
{:ok, preflight} = MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{input: "Refund order A-1001"})

Map.take(preflight.params, [:order_id, :refund, :limits])
```

The result is
`%{order_id: nil, refund: %{max_cents: 20000}, limits: %{elapsed_ms: 60000, total_tokens: 2000, total_cost: nil}}`.
Defaults merge into nested objects field by field; the Flow schema fills the rest.

```elixir
{:error, error} = MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(%{input: "Refund it", channel: "email"})

Exception.message(error)
```

The result is `"unrecognized key: channel"`.

## Run a Refund

Queue one decision and send the request:

```elixir
MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(
    ~s({"decision":"refund","amount_cents":4500,"message":"Refunded 45.00 USD for the broken blender."})
  )
])

agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "refund-desk-1")

signal =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "The blender in order A-1001 arrived broken. Please refund it.",
    order_id: "A-1001",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, next_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

Map.take(reply.data, [:status, :output, :reason_code, :model_calls, :flow])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  status: :completed,
  output: %{
    "amount_cents" => 4500,
    "decision" => "refund",
    "message" => "Refunded 45.00 USD for the broken blender."
  },
  reason_code: :none,
  model_calls: 1,
  flow: %{id: "my_app/refund_flow", vsn: 1}
}
```

Parse the output with the same Zoi schema to get atoms and checked types:

```elixir
Zoi.parse(MyApp.RefundDecision.schema(), reply.data.output)
```

The result is
`{:ok, %{decision: :refund, message: "Refunded 45.00 USD for the broken blender.", amount_cents: 4500}}`.

## What the Model Saw

```elixir
[request] = MyApp.OfflineModel.requests()

Enum.map(request["input"], fn message ->
  {message["role"], get_in(message, ["content", Access.at(0), "text"])}
end)
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  {"system",
   "You decide refund requests for the support desk. Refund at most the order\ntotal. Deny requests that give no reason. Return one decision.\n"},
  {"user", "The blender in order A-1001 arrived broken. Please refund it."},
  {"system", "Order A-1001 is delivered. Its total is 4500 cents."}
]
```

```elixir
{request["model"], request["text"]["format"]["strict"], request["text"]["format"]["schema"]["required"]}
```

The result is `{"gpt-4o-mini", true, ["message", "decision", "amount_cents"]}`.
No `tools` key is sent, because the round passes `tools: false`.

The Session has the user message and the accepted decision:

```elixir
{:ok, messages} = Jido.AI.Thread.Projection.messages(next_agent.state.session)

Enum.map(messages, & &1.role)
```

The result is `[:user, :assistant]`.

## Blocked Before the Model

A request with no `order_id` stops in `CheckPolicy`. No reply is queued,
because no model call happens:

```elixir
no_order =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Please refund my blender.",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, blocked_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: blocked}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, no_order)

Map.take(blocked.data, [:status, :output, :reason_code, :reason, :model_calls])
```

The result is
`%{status: :blocked, output: "", reason_code: :policy, reason: "a refund request needs an order_id", model_calls: 0}`.

Order A-2002 totals 89,000 cents, above this Agent's 20,000-cent limit:

```elixir
large =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Refund the espresso machine in order A-2002.",
    order_id: "A-2002",
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: blocked}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, large)

{blocked.data.reason_code, blocked.data.reason}
```

The result is
`{:policy, "order total 89000 cents is above the desk limit of 20000 cents"}`.

A blocked turn still commits: `StartRequest` appended the user message, and
`FinalizeTurn` recorded the outcome.

```elixir
Enum.map(blocked_agent.state.session.thread.entries, & &1.kind)
```

The result is `[:ai_message, :ai_request_outcome]`.

The built-in `GuardInput` still runs first. An empty `input` is blocked with
`reason_code: :input` before your policy reads the order:

```elixir
{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: empty}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(
    agent,
    MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{input: " ", order_id: "A-1001"})
  )

{empty.data.reason_code, empty.data.reason}
```

The result is `{:input, "input is empty"}`.

Schema validation runs before the Flow. A field of the wrong type returns an
error, not a reply, and commits nothing:

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(
    agent,
    MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{input: "Refund it", order_id: 1001})
  )

Exception.message(error)
```

The result is `"invalid type: expected string, at order_id"`.

## Blocked After the Model

Here the model offers more than the order total:

```elixir
MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(
    ~s({"decision":"refund","amount_cents":9000,"message":"Refunded 90.00 USD."})
  )
])

{:ok, over_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: over}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

Map.take(over.data, [:status, :output, :reason_code, :reason, :model_calls])
```

The result is
`%{status: :blocked, output: "", reason_code: :policy, reason: "refund of 9000 cents exceeds the order total of 4500 cents", model_calls: 1}`.

The model call is recorded, but the decision isn't in the conversation:

```elixir
{:ok, messages} = Jido.AI.Thread.Projection.messages(over_agent.state.session)
[attempt] = over.data.model_attempts

{Enum.map(messages, & &1.role), attempt.output_allowed, attempt.output_reason}
```

The result is
`{[:user], false, "refund of 9000 cents exceeds the order total of 4500 cents"}`.

Output that breaks the decision schema fails inside ReqLLM before your checks
run. `Complete` maps it to `:failed` with `reason_code: :model`:

```elixir
MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(~s({"decision":"maybe","amount_cents":4500,"message":"Hmm."}))
])

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: invalid}]} =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

{invalid.data.status, invalid.data.reason_code,
 String.starts_with?(invalid.data.reason, "Structured output failed final validation:")}
```

The result is `{:failed, :model, true}`.

## Budgets

`StartRequest` receives the budgets, and `FinalizeTurn` reports them:

```elixir
reply.data.budget.limits
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  elapsed_ms: 60000,
  input_tokens: nil,
  model_calls: 1,
  output_tokens: nil,
  tool_calls: 0,
  tool_rounds: 0,
  total_cost: nil,
  total_tokens: 2000
}
```

`CallModel` checks them with `Jido.AI.Budget.before_model/2` before it sends a
request. A request override of a nested object replaces the Agent default
for that object, so send every field you want to keep:

```elixir
tight =
  MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Refund order A-1001, the blender is broken.",
    order_id: "A-1001",
    limits: %{elapsed_ms: 5_000},
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, tight_preflight} = MyApp.SupportAgent.preflight(tight.data)
tight_preflight.params.limits
```

The result is `%{elapsed_ms: 5000, total_tokens: nil, total_cost: nil}`:
the Agent's `total_tokens 2_000` is gone for this request.

A spent budget gives `CallModel` a `status: :budget` outcome without a model
call, and `Complete` maps it to `:blocked` with `reason_code: :limits`. This
Flow allows one model call, so only a time budget can run out before it. A
multi-call Flow checks again before each call; see
[Budgets, Retries, and Fallbacks](budgets-retries-and-fallbacks.md).

## Reuse a Built-in Round

The expander can continue into any Flow, including a built-in round. Swap
`Route` and `Expand` for `Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.DecideInput` and
`Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.ExpandNext`, and the model work after your
policy is the full ReAct loop: tool rounds, repair, retries, and fallback
models, driven by the `max_*` params you give `StartRequest`.

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.RefundLoopFlow do
  @moduledoc "Refund policy in front of the ReAct model loop."

  alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.Actions.{DecideInput, FinalizeTurn, GuardInput, StartRequest}
  alias Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.ExpandNext

  use Jido.Flow,
    id: "my_app/refund_loop_flow",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "my_app_refund_loop_flow",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(
        %{
          input: Zoi.string(),
          order_id: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.nullable() |> Zoi.default(nil),
          options: Zoi.map() |> Zoi.default(%{max_tokens: 256, temperature: 0.0})
        },
        unrecognized_keys: :error
      ),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{state: Zoi.map(), response: Zoi.map()})

  flow do
    step "start_request",
      action: StartRequest,
      params: %{
        flow: %{id: "my_app/refund_loop_flow", vsn: 1},
        input: input(:input),
        instructions: "Decide the refund request.",
        model: "openai:gpt-4o-mini",
        options: input(:options),
        context_window: %{max_messages: 32, max_bytes: 100_000},
        max_model_calls: 2,
        max_retries: 1,
        max_tool_rounds: 0,
        max_tool_calls: 1
      }

    step "input_guard",
      action: GuardInput,
      params: %{turn: result("start_request")}

    step "policy",
      action: MyApp.Refund.CheckPolicy,
      params: %{guard: result("input_guard"), order_id: input(:order_id), max_cents: 20_000}

    invoke "loop",
      decision: DecideInput,
      expander: ExpandNext,
      params: %{turn: result("start_request"), guard: result("policy")}

    step "finalize",
      action: FinalizeTurn,
      params: %{outcome: result("loop")}

    output result("finalize")
  end
end
```

`DecideInput` reads only `allowed`, `reason_code`, and `reason` from the
guard, so `CheckPolicy`'s extra `order` key does no harm. Run it with an empty
first answer; the ReAct output guard rejects it and repairs within the budget:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LoopAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, name: "loop_agent", id: "my_app/loop_agent", vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning MyApp.RefundLoopFlow
  end
end

MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(""),
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text("Refund approved for order A-1001.")
])

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: looped}]} =
  MyApp.LoopAgent.cmd(
    MyApp.LoopAgent.instantiate!(id: "loop-1"),
    MyApp.LoopAgent.request_signal!(%{
      input: "Refund order A-1001.",
      order_id: "A-1001",
      options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
    })
  )

_requests = MyApp.OfflineModel.requests()
Map.take(looped.data, [:status, :output, :model_calls, :retries])
```

The result is
`%{status: :completed, output: "Refund approved for order A-1001.", model_calls: 2, retries: 1}`.

The trade-off: `ReAct.ModelRound` records each accepted answer before
returning, so a check that runs after the loop can block the reply but can't
keep the answer out of the Session. Put checks that must gate the
conversation inside your own round, as `MyApp.RefundRound` does.

## Human Approval

A refund over the desk limit could wait for a supervisor instead of ending
the turn. A Jido Flow wait (`await`, `timer`, or `selection`) does that, with
two rules:

1. `Jido.Exec.run/4` and `cmd/2` reject a Flow that contains a wait before any
   Action runs. Put the wait in a separate Flow and reach it through an
   `invoke`, the way `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.ToolReview` does.
2. Only a durable `Jido.AgentServer` turn supports waits: start Jido with a
   stable `namespace:` and the Agent with `persistence:`. Check
   `Jido.Exec.supports?(context, :waits)` in your decision Action and block
   with a clear reason when it returns `false`, the way `GuardInput` blocks
   tool review.

A sketch of the approval Flow:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.RefundApproval do
  use Jido.Flow,
    id: "my_app/refund_approval",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "my_app_refund_approval",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{turn: Zoi.any(), order: Zoi.map()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "request",
      action: MyApp.Refund.RequestApproval,
      params: %{turn: input(:turn), order: input(:order)}

    selection "decision" do
      await "approved",
        event: "my_app.refund.approved",
        match: %{review_id: result("request", :review_id)}

      await "denied",
        event: "my_app.refund.denied",
        match: %{review_id: result("request", :review_id)}

      timer "expired", after_ms: 900_000
    end

    invoke "next",
      decision: MyApp.Refund.ResolveApproval,
      expander: MyApp.Refund.Expand,
      params: %{turn: result("request", :turn), decision: result("decision")}

    output result("next")
  end
end
```

`MyApp.Refund.RequestApproval` publishes the request, and
`MyApp.Refund.ResolveApproval` maps the selected option to a model round or a
terminal result. Both are yours to write.
[Tool Review and Cycle Limits](tool-review-and-cycle-limits.livemd) runs the
built-in version of this pattern on a live AgentServer, and the Jido Action
[Flow Waits](https://hexdocs.pm/jido_action/flow-waits.html) guide lists
the wait result shapes.

## Inline Flows

For a small Agent, write the root Flow inside the `ai` block. It uses the
same Flow language, and its output has the same `%{state, response}` shape:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.RefundTriageAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent, name: "refund_triage_agent", id: "my_app/refund_triage_agent", vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning do
      step "triage", order_id <- input(:order_id), inline: [context: context] do
        response =
          case MyApp.Orders.fetch(order_id) do
            {:ok, %{total_cents: total}} when total <= 20_000 ->
              %{status: :completed, output: "Order #{order_id} qualifies for desk refunds."}

            _other ->
              %{status: :blocked, output: "", reason: "send this request to a supervisor"}
          end

        {:ok, %{state: context.agent_state, response: response}}
      end

      output result("triage")
    end
  end
end

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: triage}]} =
  MyApp.RefundTriageAgent.cmd(
    MyApp.RefundTriageAgent.instantiate!(id: "triage-1"),
    MyApp.RefundTriageAgent.request_signal!(%{order_id: "A-2002"})
  )

triage.data
```

The result is
`%{status: :blocked, output: "", reason: "send this request to a supervisor"}`.

| | Custom Flow module | Inline Flow |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Input schema and `reasoning` defaults | Yes | No; any map passes |
| Stable `id` and `vsn` for stored Agents | Yes | No; the module name is a generated hash |
| Reusable across Agents | Yes | No |
| Use for | Production desks, shared policy | Prototypes, tests, fixed logic |

[Agent Authoring](agent-authoring.md) covers inline Flows in full.

## Test the Flow

Test the guardrails and the exact request the way you ran them here. This
ExUnit module runs in the notebook:

```elixir
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)

defmodule MyApp.RefundFlowTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  setup do
    {:ok, agent: MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "refund-test")}
  end

  defp run(agent, data) do
    signal =
      MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(Map.put(data, :options, MyApp.OfflineModel.options()))

    {:ok, next, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
      MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

    {next, reply.data}
  end

  test "blocks a missing order without a model call", %{agent: agent} do
    {_next, data} = run(agent, %{input: "Refund my blender."})

    assert %{status: :blocked, reason_code: :policy, model_calls: 0} = data
    assert MyApp.OfflineModel.requests() == []
  end

  test "sends the order facts and the strict decision schema", %{agent: agent} do
    MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
      MyApp.OfflineModel.text(~s({"decision":"deny","amount_cents":0,"message":"No reason given."}))
    ])

    {_next, data} = run(agent, %{input: "Refund A-1001.", order_id: "A-1001"})
    [request] = MyApp.OfflineModel.requests()

    assert data.status == :completed
    assert request["model"] == "gpt-4o-mini"
    assert request["text"]["format"]["strict"] == true

    assert Enum.map(request["input"], & &1["role"]) == ["system", "user", "system"]

    assert List.last(request["input"])["content"] == [
             %{"type" => "input_text", "text" => "Order A-1001 is delivered. Its total is 4500 cents."}
           ]
  end

  test "keeps an over-total refund out of the Session", %{agent: agent} do
    MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
      MyApp.OfflineModel.text(~s({"decision":"refund","amount_cents":4501,"message":"Done."}))
    ])

    {next, data} = run(agent, %{input: "Refund A-1001.", order_id: "A-1001"})
    _requests = MyApp.OfflineModel.requests()

    assert %{status: :blocked, reason_code: :policy, model_calls: 1} = data
    assert {:ok, [%{role: :user}]} = Jido.AI.Thread.Projection.messages(next.state.session)
  end
end

ExUnit.run()
```

The result is `%{total: 3, failures: 0, excluded: 0, skipped: 0}`.

In a project, put the Agent and Flow in `lib/`, the offline model in
`test/support/`, and call `Req.Test.verify_on_exit!/0` in `setup` so an unused
queued reply fails the test. To assert on the exact `ReqLLM.Context` and
options instead of the HTTP body, see
[Mock Model Calls with ReqLLM](mock-model-calls-with-reqllm.livemd).

## Summary

- A root reasoning Flow is any Flow whose input schema has `input` and whose
  output is `%{state: map(), response: map()}`. Its schema owns request fields
  and the fields a `reasoning MyFlow do ... end` block can default.
- `StartRequest`, `GuardInput`, `ProjectConversation`, `CallModel`,
  `GuardOutput`, `RecordModelAttempt`, and `FinalizeTurn` are public Actions;
  wire them with `step`, `invoke`, and `choice`.
- A pre-model check plus an `invoke` ends the turn as `:blocked` with
  `model_calls: 0`.
- A post-model check placed before `RecordModelAttempt` keeps a rejected
  answer out of the Session; a `choice` on a declared Boolean picks the
  terminal result.
- Budgets are `StartRequest` params; `CallModel` enforces them before each
  call.
- An expander can continue into a built-in round, such as
  `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.ModelRound`, through
  `Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct.Actions.ExpandNext`.
- Waits belong in an invoked Flow and need a durable AgentServer turn.

